{"author":"rebar review bot","author_email":"rebar-review-bot@rebar.solutions.navateam.com","data":{"body":"## Closing evidence — all 12 acceptance criteria verified\n\nThe remedy landed as Gerrit change **3022**, commit\n`ff088a30ea1f7e764cd29f2d89ccb3258b5e16d6`, carrying this ticket's\n`rebar-ticket: 3057-4d76-9ee4-4817` trailer. The change took `LLM-Review +1`\nand `Verified +1` and was submitted revision-scoped against that exact SHA. It\ntouches three files: `infra/scripts/docker-storage-cap.sh`,\n`infra/terraform/monitoring_autodeploy.tf`, and\n`tests/scripts/test_docker_storage_cap.py`.\n\n### Behavioural criteria — pinned by tests\n\nThe schema-selection criteria, the read-back criteria, the no-restart\ncriterion, and the sibling-key preservation criterion are all behavioural, and\nevery one is covered by `tests/scripts/test_docker_storage_cap.py`, which\nexits 0 against the merged tree: **34 passed in 21.67s**.\n\nThat covers: schema chosen from the BuildKit version rather than the Engine\nversion; Engine 25.0.16 with BuildKit 0.13+ still selecting `modern`; an\nunreadable BuildKit version still rendering `modern`; BuildKit 0.12.6\nrendering `defaultKeepStorage` and not `maxUsedSpace`; the read-back warning on\nmismatch; silence on a match; no automatic Docker restart; and sibling keys\ninside and outside `builder` surviving the rewrite.\n\n### Alarm-description criterion — read from the merged diff\n\nThe old text asserted the Engine rule directly. The merged text removes that\nassertion, attributes the key to BuildKit, and separates the loaded policy from\nthe effective one:\n\n```\n-    Engine >= 25.0, defaultKeepStorage below) and that the daemon reloaded. Published as\n+    enforced ceiling is BuildKit's default. That key is a BUILDKIT feature, not an Engine one\n+    (maxUsedSpace at BuildKit >= 0.13, defaultKeepStorage below), and the Engine version does\n+    not imply the BuildKit version — bug 3057: Engine 25.0.16, BuildKit 0.12.6. Re-run\n+    docker-storage-cap.sh --install; it reports effective keep-bytes and warns on mismatch.\n```\n\n### Portability criterion\n\nThe fix is a host-side shell script plus its Terraform alarm text and its unit\ntests. Nothing in the changed code is triggered by, or conditioned on, GitHub\nActions or any other single CI provider — `docker-storage-cap.sh --install` is\ninvoked on the host, and the schema decision is made in-process from the\nBuildKit version it reads there. There is no CI-provider dependency to fall\nback from.\n\n### `make verify` criterion\n\nRun in this ticket's worktree, `make verify` showed **exactly 17 failures, all\npandoc `Bad CPU type in executable`** — 13 in `test_wiki_render.py`, 3 in\n`test_rich_text_cutover.py`, and 1 in\n`diffing/test_diff_comments_cutover_neutral.py`. That is the known macOS-arm64\nenvironmental set for this host and nothing outside it failed, which is what\nthis criterion asks for. The Linux side is covered independently by the\n`Verified +1` on revision `ff088a30ea1f`.\n\n### Root cause, restated\n\nThe cap key was selected from the **Docker Engine** version on the assumption\nthat Engine >= 25.0 implies BuildKit >= 0.13. On the live host that assumption\nwas false — Engine 25.0.16 shipped with BuildKit 0.12.6 — so the script wrote\n`maxUsedSpace`, a key that BuildKit 0.12.6 does not understand. BuildKit\nignored it, fell back to its own default ceiling, never reclaimed, and the\nalarm stayed latched for eight days while the configuration looked correct to\nanyone reading `daemon.json`. Keying the schema off BuildKit's own version\nremoves the inference, and the read-back turns a silently ignored key into a\nwarning at install time."},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1789943373878668001,"uuid":"c6e1181f-fd9c-48d0-b1d9-e63d5d871f52"}